  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 115 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nSynopsis: Cooler temperatures will prevail during the day Monday in \nthe wake of the Sunday evening/night cold frontal passage. Southerly \nto eventually southwesterly surface flow will steadily return \nTuesday into Wednesday, allowing for a steady warm up by the middle \nof the work week. Currently centered off the British Columbia coast, \nan upper trough will slowly progress from the Cascades into the \nnorthern Rockies Monday into Tuesday. The trough will eject into the \nNorthern Plains Tuesday night, quickly progressing toward the \nsouthern Hudson Bay vicinity by Thursday morning. The trough will \ninduce surface cyclogenesis over the Dakotas Tuesday night, with the \ndeveloping surface low moving northeast toward the Hudson Bay by \nThursday. The low will pull a cold front across southern Wisconsin \nsome point Wednesday afternoon into Thursday morning, with forcing \nalong the front providing chances for light rain. Mid-level height \nfalls will slowly but steadily build into the Upper Mississippi \nValley next weekend, supporting additional rain potential. \n\nMonday night into Tuesday: Forecast continues to trend rain-free as \nan upper shortwave steadily trends along a more northwesterly \ntrack/away from southern Wisconsin. With modeled mid-level height \nfalls & low level frontogenesis and temp advection signals \ncontinuing to shift away from the area, emerging QPF-free forecasts \nfrom the NBM thus appear reasonable & have been maintained in the \nafternoon update. Will continue to monitor trends in coming updates.\n\nWednesday afternoon into Thursday morning: In contrast to 24 hours \nago, global deterministic and ensemble guidance is coming into much \nbetter agreement regarding large scale pattern evolution. Confidence \nin a weak cold frontal passage is thus increasing during this time \nframe, with modeled precipitable water & moisture transport \nsupportive of at least some light rain potential. Limiting factor \nwill be the magnitude of available frontal convergence, as the \nboundary may be entering a decaying phase as it arrives into \nsouthern Wisconsin. As such, NBM's current ~20-40% precip \nprobabilities appear representative to the setup, and will be \niterated off of as this portion of the period draws closer. Initial \nprobabilistic QPF points toward totals remaining at or below a tenth \nof an inch.\n\nQuigley",
